WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.240 --> 00:00:07.560
Of all the jurisdictions in the&nbsp;
world, there is one jurisdiction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.560 --> 00:00:10.560
that is following what is happening&nbsp;
in Ukraine with special attention,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:14.040
every minute, every day, and&nbsp;
that is none other than Taiwan.

00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:15.900
An island that seems to be ticking all the boxes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:19.080
for becoming the next protagonist&nbsp;
of a major international crisis.
 &nbsp;

00:00:19.080 --> 00:00:23.340
With the protection of the United States for&nbsp;
more than 70 years, Taiwan has lived on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.340 --> 00:00:27.780
fringes of the Chinese regime, but always&nbsp;
with the latent threat that one day Beijing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.780 --> 00:00:32.700
might ultimately try to invade the island.
 
Of course, this hypothesis, this possibility&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:37.020
could trigger a conflict that would go far&nbsp;
beyond an armed clash on a regional scale.
 &nbsp;

00:00:37.020 --> 00:00:41.100
It would be the first time since the Vietnam&nbsp;
War that the US and China could potentially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:45.840
face off in a theater of operations... Or at&nbsp;
least that's the usual impression one gets,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:59.400
if only from statements like these:
 
The question is, what could happen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:04.380
if the conflict in Taiwan finally erupts? Would&nbsp;
China succeed in taking control of this island?

00:01:04.380 --> 00:01:05.880
Let's get started.

00:01:17.220 --> 00:01:20.880
We have mentioned this lot of times:&nbsp;
Taiwan's situation is one of the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:24.540
ambiguous in international politics.&nbsp;
Particularly because the sovereignty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.540 --> 00:01:29.340
of this island has undergone constant changes.
 
For example, and this may surprise you, when the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.340 --> 00:01:36.120
Sino-Japanese war of 1895 ended, the Qing Empire&nbsp;
ceded the island in perpetuity to Japan. That is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.120 --> 00:01:40.980
formally, the island of Formosa, Taiwan, ceased&nbsp;
to be China. And so, for the next 50 years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.980 --> 00:01:45.960
this island was part of the Empire of Japan.
 
Of course, Tokyo's defeat in the second World War&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.960 --> 00:01:51.300
again brought about a turn of events. The island&nbsp;
once again became part of the then Republic of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.300 --> 00:01:56.280
China, a country ruled by a military dictatorship&nbsp;
and somewhat aligned with the United States.
 &nbsp;

00:01:56.280 --> 00:02:01.440
However, when the revolution of Mao&nbsp;
Zedong and company succeeded in 1949,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:05.880
the authoritarian regime of the&nbsp;
Republic of China was exiled to Taiwan.
 &nbsp;

00:02:05.880 --> 00:02:10.920
Since then, the island has been considered&nbsp;
by Beijing as a kind of "rebel province". An&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.920 --> 00:02:15.720
island that is fully autonomous and functions&nbsp;
in practice as a sovereign country, but which,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.720 --> 00:02:18.660
however, has never been able&nbsp;
to declare its independence.

00:02:18.660 --> 00:02:26.520
Firstly because its government considered&nbsp;
itself the legitimate government of China,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.520 --> 00:02:30.300
and later because Beijing has&nbsp;
said ad nauseam, again and again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.300 --> 00:02:34.200
that any declaration of independence&nbsp;
would be considered an act of war.
 &nbsp;

00:02:34.200 --> 00:02:38.460
And so it has been molded in this way,&nbsp;
a country that over recent decades has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.460 --> 00:02:44.280
experienced an important economic, technological&nbsp;
and, also, democratic success. In political and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.280 --> 00:02:48.900
social terms, Taiwan has become the freest&nbsp;
territory on the entire Asian continent.
 &nbsp;

00:02:48.900 --> 00:02:53.340
The problem... China's appetite is getting bigger&nbsp;
and bigger. And, if the war in Ukraine has shown&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.340 --> 00:02:57.540
us anything, it is that the territorial&nbsp;
claims of the major powers are once again&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.540 --> 00:03:02.760
a feasible scenario for military conflict.
 
The question is, will Taiwan become the new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.760 --> 00:03:06.900
Ukraine? What would a Chinese invasion of the&nbsp;
island look like? Who would be involved and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.900 --> 00:03:10.200
what would be the possible outcomes?
 
Well, stick with me to find out.
&nbsp;

00:03:11.760 --> 00:03:17.640
TWO STARTING POINTS)
 
The first thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.640 --> 00:03:22.680
to keep in mind is that in the hypothesis of a&nbsp;
war between the United States and China in Taiwan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:26.220
both sides have a completely different&nbsp;
view of the situation... This reveals&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.220 --> 00:03:33.660
some key points to keep in mind.
 
For Beijing, the Taiwan issue is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.660 --> 00:03:37.980
strictly domestic one. Since the island belonged&nbsp;
to the Republic of China and the Mao revolution&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.980 --> 00:03:42.420
replaced that for the current communist regime&nbsp;
– the People's Republic of China – from their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.420 --> 00:03:47.640
point of view, Taiwan is a Chinese province&nbsp;
with a de facto degree of special autonomy.
 &nbsp;

00:03:47.640 --> 00:03:52.800
In other words, Xi Jinping's government considers&nbsp;
that any military operation would not be a clash&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:57.120
between countries, but an event relating&nbsp;
to China's internal territorial order.
 &nbsp;

00:03:57.120 --> 00:04:01.620
And, keep in mind that it’s this position that&nbsp;
explains Beijing's growing belligerence towards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.620 --> 00:04:06.420
any foreign group meddling on the island. They see&nbsp;
it as a kind of humiliation. A feeling that has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.420 --> 00:04:12.000
been reinforced by years of communist propaganda.
 
(9 October 2021: China's Xi vows 'reunification'&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:14.220
with Taiwan. – Reuters)
 
For its part, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.220 --> 00:04:19.020
the United States maintains a commitment to&nbsp;
Taiwan that is not entirely compatible with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.020 --> 00:04:24.420
this position: in the Taiwan Relations Act&nbsp;
enacted on 10 April 1979, the United States&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.420 --> 00:04:30.000
made an unwavering commitment to this territory:
 
("The United States shall make available to Taiwan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:35.640
such defense articles and defense services in such&nbsp;
quantity as may be necessary to enable Taiwan to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.640 --> 00:04:40.140
maintain sufficient self-defense capabilities"&nbsp;
– Section 3 of the Taiwan Relations Act).
 &nbsp;

00:04:40.140 --> 00:04:44.700
In other words, the law makes it very clear:&nbsp;
Taiwan must be able to defend itself against&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.700 --> 00:04:49.740
any attempt of aggression... And the United States&nbsp;
will be responsible should this situation occur.
 &nbsp;

00:04:49.740 --> 00:04:53.880
In fact, there doesn't seem to be much point in&nbsp;
talking about Taiwan's defense except with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.880 --> 00:05:04.020
US. Why? Well... Look at this.
DAVID VS. GOLIATH?)
 &nbsp;

00:05:07.500 --> 00:05:12.600
The first question we have to answer is.... If&nbsp;
China eventually does decide to invade Taiwan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.600 --> 00:05:14.820
which countries would be&nbsp;
involved in the conflict?
 &nbsp;

00:05:14.820 --> 00:05:18.720
And take note because this is where&nbsp;
the key point of US diplomacy comes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.720 --> 00:05:22.740
into play: strategic ambiguity.
 
For years, at least officially,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.740 --> 00:05:26.700
Washington has never made it clear whether&nbsp;
or not it would intervene in that possible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.700 --> 00:05:31.080
conflict. And it has not done so in order to&nbsp;
create a double deterrent: on the one hand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.080 --> 00:05:35.640
the latent threat of US intervention could&nbsp;
prevent China from deciding to invade the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.640 --> 00:05:40.320
island. On the other hand, the uncertainty also&nbsp;
does not allow Taipei to be overly aggressive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.320 --> 00:05:44.580
for example, by declaring its independence.
 
This is the approach that, according to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.580 --> 00:05:48.240
many analysts, has succeeded in&nbsp;
maintaining peace for decades.
 &nbsp;

00:05:48.240 --> 00:05:51.600
However, things are evolving, the&nbsp;
Chinese military is increasingly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.600 --> 00:05:55.560
prepared and if the war in Ukraine has made&nbsp;
anything clear, it is that the strategy of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.560 --> 00:06:00.480
strategic ambiguity could end up being Xi&nbsp;
Jinping's excuse to pounce on his prey.
 &nbsp;

00:06:00.480 --> 00:06:04.500
That is to say, if at any given moment,&nbsp;
top Chinese officials thought that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.500 --> 00:06:08.640
United States might not come to Taiwan's&nbsp;
rescue and may settle, for instance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.640 --> 00:06:13.380
with the application of sanctions, then that might&nbsp;
be the best fuse for unleashing an invasion.
 &nbsp;

00:06:13.380 --> 00:06:16.800
This is similar to what happened with&nbsp;
the Russian invasion of Ukraine. Putin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.800 --> 00:06:21.000
and his entourage felt that when push came to&nbsp;
shove, no one would lift a finger for Kyiv.
 &nbsp;

00:06:21.000 --> 00:06:26.400
And that is what explains why Joe Biden has&nbsp;
intentionally "confused" himself not once, not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.400 --> 00:06:31.500
twice, but three times, making it clear that the&nbsp;
United States would come to the aid of its ally.&nbsp;

00:07:01.140 --> 00:07:06.240
In other words, Washington has not changed its&nbsp;
formal position, but the president wanted to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.240 --> 00:07:10.320
make clear the position of the White House.
 
Perhaps it has a lot to do with the fact that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.320 --> 00:07:15.120
the advanced semiconductor industry has made&nbsp;
Taiwan an absolutely strategic enclave for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.120 --> 00:07:19.860
the United States. American companies, American&nbsp;
technology, and it doesn't matter whether we're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.860 --> 00:07:25.920
talking about artificial intelligence, Amazon&nbsp;
or the F35, they all need chips made by TSMC.
 &nbsp;

00:07:25.920 --> 00:07:28.200
[I don't know for certain&nbsp;
but I'm sure that further&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.200 --> 00:07:32.340
supports the resolve of the United States].
 
But why is the position of American power so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.340 --> 00:07:38.280
important? Well, basically because American power&nbsp;
is Taipei's only asset. Why? Well, take a look.&nbsp;

00:07:40.980 --> 00:07:48.360
The de-correlation of forces between Taiwan and&nbsp;
the People's Republic of China has continued to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.360 --> 00:07:52.500
grow over time. If the Chinese armed forces&nbsp;
have traditionally been a poorly equipped,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.500 --> 00:07:56.580
poorly trained and poorly capable army,&nbsp;
the situation is very different today.
 &nbsp;

00:07:56.580 --> 00:08:00.600
The People's Republic of China is making&nbsp;
an enormous effort to improve its military&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.600 --> 00:08:05.940
capabilities, including the launching of six&nbsp;
new aircraft carriers, 5th generation fighters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.940 --> 00:08:10.800
new amphibious capabilities and thousands&nbsp;
and thousands of missiles aimed at Taiwan.
 &nbsp;

00:08:10.800 --> 00:08:15.420
Yes, it is true that Taiwan concentrates its&nbsp;
military spending on protecting a much smaller&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.420 --> 00:08:21.360
territory: 36,000km2 compared to more than&nbsp;
9 million. But China is directing the bulk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.360 --> 00:08:24.600
of its military investments towards&nbsp;
maritime dominance in the region,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.600 --> 00:08:29.160
including the ability to invade Taiwan.
 
In fact, the widening gap in forces&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.160 --> 00:08:33.060
may be the reason Taiwan has reduced its&nbsp;
military effort over the past few decades:
 &nbsp;

00:08:33.060 --> 00:08:37.440
The idea that a China that is more open&nbsp;
to the world could eliminate any risk of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.440 --> 00:08:41.700
invasion and that ultimately everything&nbsp;
will depend on the US position is perhaps&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.700 --> 00:08:47.700
what explains charts like this one:
Now, this is something that could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.700 --> 00:08:52.320
begin to change, but the truth is that without&nbsp;
international assistance, Taiwan would have a very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.320 --> 00:08:57.180
difficult time resisting an invasion by China.
 
(17 June 2022: China launches the most advanced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.180 --> 00:09:01.980
aircraft carrier on the planet outside the US.&nbsp;
China strikes a new military blow with its new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.980 --> 00:09:06.120
aircraft carrier type 003. – El Confidencial)
 
(15 April 2023: Taiwan highly vulnerable to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.120 --> 00:09:10.080
Chinese air attack, leaked documents show
Troubling details raise questions about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.080 --> 00:09:14.400
U.S. intelligence agencies' ability to&nbsp;
detect a pending invasion and the island's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.400 --> 00:09:18.000
capacity to defend itself. – Washington Post)
 
Now, let's suppose that, because of X or Y,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.000 --> 00:09:23.340
everything finally goes wrong, things get out&nbsp;
of control and the conflict explodes: What could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.340 --> 00:09:27.840
happen? What would be the balance of forces? Would&nbsp;
any of the contenders clearly impose themselves?
 &nbsp;

00:09:27.840 --> 00:09:34.920
Well... Let’s take a look.
 
(T3. WAR GAMES)
 &nbsp;

00:09:34.920 --> 00:09:44.040
What could happen if war ultimately does break&nbsp;
out in Taiwan? It is not such a far-fetched&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.040 --> 00:09:49.260
hypothesis, so in Washington it is something&nbsp;
they take very, very seriously. Precisely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.260 --> 00:09:54.180
for this reason, several US research and defense&nbsp;
centers have carried out simulations to find out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.180 --> 00:09:58.560
how this possible conflict would develop.
 
In virtually all cases it is taken for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.560 --> 00:10:03.600
granted that US forces would participate,&nbsp;
otherwise Taiwan's defeat would be certain.
 &nbsp;

00:10:03.600 --> 00:10:07.680
Well, let's take, for example, as a starting&nbsp;
point the simulation carried out by the Center&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.680 --> 00:10:11.700
for Strategic and International Studies,&nbsp;
a leading Washington-based think tank.
 &nbsp;

00:10:11.700 --> 00:10:16.140
In order to carry out this simulation, all&nbsp;
possible air and naval actions were taken into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.140 --> 00:10:20.760
account, as well as all historical precedents&nbsp;
with amphibious missions, from Normandy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.760 --> 00:10:25.200
to Okinawa, to the Falkland Islands.
 
The results? Well... Take a look.
 &nbsp;

00:10:25.200 --> 00:10:30.480
In most scenarios, the United States, Taiwan&nbsp;
and Japan would succeed in defeating China's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.480 --> 00:10:34.380
conventional amphibious invasion and&nbsp;
maintain Taiwan's autonomy. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.380 --> 00:10:39.300
this defense would come at a high cost. The United&nbsp;
States and its allies would lose dozens of ships,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.300 --> 00:10:44.340
hundreds of aircraft and suffer tens of thousands&nbsp;
of casualties. Just think, the US would have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.340 --> 00:10:48.840
fight thousands of miles away against a Chinese&nbsp;
military that would be playing on home soil.&nbsp;

00:10:48.840 --> 00:10:53.580
For its part, Taiwan's economy would be devastated&nbsp;
by the destruction of much of its infrastructure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.580 --> 00:10:56.640
of course China would also suffer&nbsp;
great losses and the failure of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.640 --> 00:11:00.120
the operation could end up destabilizing&nbsp;
the Chinese Communist Party government.
 &nbsp;

00:11:00.120 --> 00:11:08.340
The results of this simulation show that&nbsp;
only when Taiwan is left alone would China&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.340 --> 00:11:13.020
succeed in taking control of the island.
 
However, even in this scenario, Taiwanese&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.020 --> 00:11:17.640
resistance could extend the invasion by 70 days.
 
Okay, I know what some of you are thinking:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.640 --> 00:11:22.920
There you go again, VisualPolitik, propagandizing&nbsp;
for the imperialist dominance of Uncle Sam! Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.920 --> 00:11:25.860
we are not doing it now, nor&nbsp;
have we ever done it before.
 &nbsp;

00:11:25.860 --> 00:11:29.520
In fact, these drills are anything but&nbsp;
encouraging for the United States.
 &nbsp;

00:11:29.520 --> 00:11:34.080
In the simulation, even the best-case&nbsp;
scenario entails extremely significant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.080 --> 00:11:38.220
losses for the United States and Japan,&nbsp;
as well as major economic repercussions.

00:11:38.220 --> 00:11:43.860
In other words: no matter what the outcome, no one&nbsp;
would win. Any Chinese attempt to invade Taiwan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.860 --> 00:11:46.980
would be a disaster for all parties.
 
But that's not all.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.340 --> 00:11:54.480
The results of this simulation warn&nbsp;
of three more than possible risks:
 &nbsp;

00:11:54.480 --> 00:11:58.860
Firstly, the damage caused by cyber attacks&nbsp;
could cause significant economic and social&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:58.860 --> 00:12:03.060
damage in the United States itself.
 
Secondly, the damage sustained by US&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:03.060 --> 00:12:08.160
military forces could be difficult to replace.&nbsp;
Rebuilding capability could take years, while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:08.160 --> 00:12:13.320
China, a country endowed with greater industrial&nbsp;
capacity, could take advantage of such a scenario&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:13.320 --> 00:12:17.460
to turn the tables. It would be something&nbsp;
of a Pyrrhic victory for the United States.
 &nbsp;

00:12:17.460 --> 00:12:21.960
Thirdly, countries such as Russia, North&nbsp;
Korea and Iran could take advantage of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:21.960 --> 00:12:26.580
this distraction and drain on US resources&nbsp;
to accelerate their political agendas.
 &nbsp;

00:12:26.580 --> 00:12:31.020
In other words, even if the United States&nbsp;
could avoid Taiwan's annexation today,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.020 --> 00:12:38.880
the potential risks would be very high.
 
Sometimes in war, winning is not everything.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:38.880 --> 00:12:43.380
History teaches us important lessons&nbsp;
in this regard. For example, in 1919,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:43.380 --> 00:12:48.300
after the signing of the Treaty of Versailles,&nbsp;
French Marshal Ferdinand Foch expressed his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:48.300 --> 00:12:52.440
concern about the outcome of the first World&nbsp;
War, "We have signed a 20-year armistice."
 &nbsp;

00:12:52.440 --> 00:12:56.520
The Marshal considered that despite having&nbsp;
won the war, France had been seriously&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.520 --> 00:13:01.140
decimated and could be vulnerable to a German&nbsp;
rearmament in the not too distant future. Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:01.140 --> 00:13:04.680
the simulations suggest that something very&nbsp;
similar could happen to the United States.
 &nbsp;

00:13:08.280 --> 00:13:12.720
And, keep in mind that the consequences of&nbsp;
this issue could become much more complex...&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:12.720 --> 00:13:16.920
Even without the outbreak of war as&nbsp;
we all conceive it. In January 2022,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:16.920 --> 00:13:20.160
another think tank, the Center&nbsp;
for a New American Security,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:20.160 --> 00:13:24.300
ran a simulation to see what might&nbsp;
happen to the semiconductor industry.

00:13:24.300 --> 00:13:26.280
The results were not optimistic.

00:13:26.280 --> 00:13:31.200
Similarly, in June 2022, this same think&nbsp;
tank also conducted a simulation of how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:31.200 --> 00:13:34.440
the war might unfold. In this&nbsp;
case, just before the invasion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:34.440 --> 00:13:39.420
the dilemma facing Beijing would be whether&nbsp;
to concentrate exclusively on Taiwan and avoid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:39.420 --> 00:13:43.680
attacking US forces in the hope that they&nbsp;
would stand aside or, on the other hand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:43.680 --> 00:13:48.000
attack the Americans to gain military&nbsp;
advantage and try to force a negotiation.
 &nbsp;

00:13:48.000 --> 00:13:51.960
The curious thing is that according to&nbsp;
this simulation, in the end China would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:51.960 --> 00:13:56.280
choose to attack Taiwan in parallel&nbsp;
to an offensive on nearby US bases.

00:13:56.280 --> 00:14:00.900
And guess what? The Chinese military is known to&nbsp;
be training to attack the US fleet. For example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:00.900 --> 00:14:05.400
as you can see in these images taken in a&nbsp;
desert in Xinjiang province, the Chinese are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:05.400 --> 00:14:10.680
using targets that recreate Nimitz-class aircraft&nbsp;
carriers and other US warships for their military&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:10.680 --> 00:14:19.200
missile training. Is this a huge red flag?
In fact, according to the simulations we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:19.200 --> 00:14:23.400
are discussing, one of China's options would&nbsp;
be to avoid attacking Taiwan with missiles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:23.400 --> 00:14:28.560
so as not to seriously damage its technological&nbsp;
industry, and instead detonate a nuclear device&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:28.560 --> 00:14:32.040
in the Pacific to intimidate the&nbsp;
Americans and force negotiations.

00:14:32.040 --> 00:14:36.720
This would also explain the recent rush Xi&nbsp;
Jinping seems to be in to build many more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:36.720 --> 00:14:40.080
nuclear weapons. In other words, if they&nbsp;
are really willing to use such a threat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:40.080 --> 00:14:44.820
for it to be credible and have an effect it has&nbsp;
to be backed up by the strongest possible force.
 &nbsp;

00:14:44.820 --> 00:14:49.320
(China already surpasses the U.S. in&nbsp;
ground-based nuclear offensive capability.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:49.320 --> 00:14:53.100
China already has more ground-based nuclear&nbsp;
missile launchers than the United States,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:53.100 --> 00:14:56.880
although it has not yet surpassed it in total&nbsp;
number of nuclear warheads. – El Confidencial)
 &nbsp;

00:14:56.880 --> 00:15:02.280
(China likely to have 1,500 nuclear&nbsp;
warheads by 2035: Pentagon. – Reuters)
 &nbsp;

00:15:02.280 --> 00:15:07.140
And so VisualPolitik community, in general, the&nbsp;
result of most of the simulations that have been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:07.140 --> 00:15:11.460
conducted is that the United States would&nbsp;
outperform China militarily, but that yes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:11.460 --> 00:15:16.140
it would do so by paying an excessively high&nbsp;
price for not being sufficiently prepared.

00:15:16.140 --> 00:15:19.800
And do you know what? This is where the&nbsp;
work of Secretary of State Antony Blinken&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:19.800 --> 00:15:23.520
and Secretary of Defense Lloyd Austin&nbsp;
in pushing defense agreements in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:23.520 --> 00:15:27.540
region makes all the sense in the world.
 
It is possible that the best and, perhaps,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:27.540 --> 00:15:32.220
only effective way to deter China is for the&nbsp;
United States to secure the support of many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:32.220 --> 00:15:37.560
more contenders: Japan, South Korea, Australia,&nbsp;
the United Kingdom, and so on and so forth.
 &nbsp;

00:15:37.560 --> 00:15:41.520
In other words, that in this&nbsp;
case, the US card alone may no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:41.520 --> 00:15:45.300
longer be enough of a deterrent for China.
 
And this leads directly to another question:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:45.300 --> 00:15:49.080
what have we learned and what have we not&nbsp;
learned from the Ukrainian experience?
&nbsp;

00:15:53.160 --> 00:15:56.820
KYIV'S NON-LESSON AND INSUFFICIENT VICTORY)
 &nbsp;

00:15:59.280 --> 00:16:03.060
One problem with Taiwan's defense is that&nbsp;
trying to replicate what has been done in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:03.060 --> 00:16:07.560
Ukraine to prevent foreign intervention may&nbsp;
not be feasible. The geographical situation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:07.560 --> 00:16:11.940
is completely different. I think you will be able&nbsp;
to see the differences perfectly on these maps.&nbsp;

00:16:11.940 --> 00:16:17.100
In the Ukrainian war, both the United States and&nbsp;
the other NATO allies have sent huge quantities&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:17.100 --> 00:16:21.420
of equipment and supplies by land, something&nbsp;
that would be impossible in Taiwan because of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:21.420 --> 00:16:26.160
its island status. In this case, China could&nbsp;
isolate the island for weeks or even months.
 &nbsp;

00:16:26.160 --> 00:16:31.140
In fact, on 8 April, for at least three days,&nbsp;
the Chinese military rehearsed how to conduct&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:31.140 --> 00:16:36.420
a "total encirclement" of Taiwan to cut off any&nbsp;
possibility of the United States being able to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:36.420 --> 00:16:40.260
resupply local self-defense forces.
 
It's a strategy that is reinforced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:40.260 --> 00:16:43.980
by things like this, check it out.
 
(22 August 2022: Current procurement&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:43.980 --> 00:16:48.300
and acquisition plans reveal that in ten years,&nbsp;
the United States will not have significantly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:48.300 --> 00:16:52.440
more forces to deploy in the region than&nbsp;
it has now. The U.S. Navy is modernizing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:52.440 --> 00:16:58.740
but will not have its planned fleet of between 450&nbsp;
and 500 ships until 2045, a fleet size that China&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:58.740 --> 00:17:02.940
will have in just ten years. Foreign Affairs)
 
Thus, any attempt to defend Taiwan has to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:02.940 --> 00:17:06.480
start with the island being prepared&nbsp;
to repel an invasion at any time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:06.480 --> 00:17:10.260
including having all the ammunition&nbsp;
and supplies necessary to resist.

00:17:12.480 --> 00:17:17.880
For its part, the United States must&nbsp;
have the capability to rapidly deploy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:17.880 --> 00:17:22.440
troops and equipment in or around Taiwan.
 
And do you know what? All of what we're seeing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:22.440 --> 00:17:28.020
is why Taiwan is becoming the No. 1 priority for&nbsp;
security and defense officials, not only in their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:28.020 --> 00:17:32.460
own government but also in the US Congress.
 
(20 April 2023: US war game against Taiwan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:32.460 --> 00:17:37.620
shows need for "decisive action" to increase&nbsp;
armament. A US congressional war game simulating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:37.620 --> 00:17:41.880
a Chinese invasion of Taiwan showed the need&nbsp;
to arm the island "to the teeth" [...] The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:41.880 --> 00:17:45.000
US should increase long-range missile&nbsp;
production and companies should prepare&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:45.000 --> 00:17:48.900
for the economic consequences. – Reuters)
 
But, all of this, of the possible plan to try&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:48.900 --> 00:17:53.220
to defend itself against China, we have already&nbsp;
covered in several videos, here on VisualPolitik,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:53.220 --> 00:17:57.060
so I’ll leave you the links in the description.
 
VisualPolitik viewers, the war in Ukraine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:57.060 --> 00:18:00.420
has shown us two things: that the&nbsp;
unthinkable is perfectly possible,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:00.420 --> 00:18:05.280
and that the times of great military clashes have&nbsp;
not yet been laid to rest in the history books.
 &nbsp;

00:18:05.280 --> 00:18:08.940
The US objective is to seek ways&nbsp;
to preserve Taiwan's independence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:08.940 --> 00:18:12.420
and in general, the military and&nbsp;
economic superiority of free countries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:12.420 --> 00:18:17.760
over any autocracy in the world. But in order to&nbsp;
achieve this, it may be absolutely indispensable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:17.760 --> 00:18:22.380
to enlist strong support and allies.
 
The question is: Can Taiwan's de facto&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:22.380 --> 00:18:26.880
independence really be safeguarded? Will China be&nbsp;
willing to take up arms? Will the United States&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:26.880 --> 00:18:31.500
succeed in building a grand coalition of free&nbsp;
countries willing to defend Taiwan’s freedom?
